\section{Versioned Code Generation Strategies}\label{sec:versioned-strategies}

\subsection{Polymorphic Inline Caching}\label{sec:polymorphic-ic}

Versioned code can be generated for different receiver types at a call site:

\begin{definition}[Polymorphic Inline Cache]\label{def:polymorphic-ic}
A \emph{polymorphic inline cache} (PIC) is a call site that has been specialized for multiple receiver types. Each type has a dedicated code path with the target method inlined.
\end{definition}

\begin{verbatim}
  ; PIC with 3 versions
  cmp [obj.type], STRING_TYPE
  je string_version       ; inlined String.length()
  cmp [obj.type], ARRAY_TYPE
  je array_version        ; inlined Array.length()
  cmp [obj.type], BUFFER_TYPE
  je buffer_version       ; inlined Buffer.length()
  jmp generic_dispatch    ; full dynamic dispatch
\end{verbatim}

The number of versions in a PIC is typically limited (e.g., 4--8). Beyond this limit, the call site becomes megamorphic and falls back to generic dispatch.

\subsection{Loop Versioning}\label{sec:loop-versioning}

Loops are particularly amenable to versioning because they execute many times, amortizing the cost of version selection:

\begin{example}[Versioned Loop for Array Operations]\label{ex:versioned-array}
\begin{verbatim}
  ; Version 1: float64 array, aligned, no overlap with output
  guard(isFloat64Array(arr)) -> version_2
  guard(isAligned(arr.data, 16)) -> version_2
  guard(noOverlap(arr.data, out.data)) -> version_2
  vectorized_loop:
    %v = load_aligned [arr.data + i]  ; SIMD load
    %r = fmul %v, 2.0                ; SIMD multiply
    store_aligned [out.data + i], %r  ; SIMD store
    i += 4
    if i < n goto vectorized_loop

  ; Version 2: float64 array, unaligned
  guard(isFloat64Array(arr)) -> version_3
  scalar_loop:
    out[i] = arr[i] * 2.0
    i++
    if i < n goto scalar_loop

  ; Version 3: generic (handles all element types)
  generic_loop:
    out[i] = dynamic_mul(arr[i], 2.0)
    i++
    if i < n goto generic_loop
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\subsection{Speculation Failure Counters}\label{sec:spec-counters}

When a speculation fails, the system must decide whether to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Recompile with a different speculation.
  \item Fall back to a less-specialized version.
  \item Give up on speculation for this code region.
\end{itemize}

This decision is guided by \emph{speculation failure counters}:

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\caption{Speculation Failure Handling}
\label{alg:spec-failure}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Procedure{HandleSpecFailure}{$\text{code\_region}, \text{assumption}$}
  \State $\text{assumption.fail\_count} \gets \text{assumption.fail\_count} + 1$
  \If{$\text{assumption.fail\_count} < \text{threshold\_retry}$}
    \State \textsc{Recompile}(code\_region, \text{new\_assumptions})
  \ElsIf{$\text{assumption.fail\_count} < \text{threshold\_fallback}$}
    \State \textsc{InstallFallbackVersion}(code\_region)
  \Else
    \State \textsc{InstallGenericVersion}(code\_region)
    \State \textsc{Blacklist}(code\_region, \text{assumption})
  \EndIf
\EndProcedure
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\section{Speculative IR for Static Languages}\label{sec:spec-static}

\subsection{Speculation in Statically-Typed Languages}\label{sec:spec-static-typed}

Even statically-typed languages benefit from speculative IR. Consider Java:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Final field speculation}: a \texttt{final} field is assumed to be initialized and never changed. If reflection modifies it, deoptimization occurs.
  \item \textbf{Sealed class speculation}: a sealed class hierarchy is assumed to have no new subtypes. If a new subtype is loaded, deoptimization occurs.
  \item \textbf{Non-null speculation}: a parameter annotated \texttt{@NonNull} is assumed to never be null.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Class Hierarchy Analysis and Speculation}\label{sec:cha-spec}

Class hierarchy analysis (CHA) provides static information that can be used for speculation:

\begin{definition}[CHA-Based Speculation]\label{def:cha-spec}
If class hierarchy analysis shows that a method $m$ has exactly one implementation (or zero, making it abstract), the JIT can speculatively inline that implementation, guarded by an assumption that no new subclass overriding $m$ is loaded.
\end{definition}

The assumption is registered with the class loader: when a new class is loaded that overrides $m$, all code depending on the assumption is deoptimized.

\section{Guard Optimization}\label{sec:guard-optimization}

\subsection{Guard Hoisting}\label{sec:guard-hoisting}

Guards can be moved (hoisted) to earlier points in the program to reduce the number of times they are executed:

\begin{definition}[Guard Hoisting]\label{def:guard-hoisting}
\emph{Guard hoisting} is the optimization of moving a guard to an earlier program point where it dominates more uses of the guarded assumption, reducing the total number of guard executions.
\end{definition}

\begin{verbatim}
  ; Before hoisting:
  loop:
    guard_type %x, Int -> deopt    ; executed every iteration
    %r = int_add %x, 1
    ...
    goto loop

  ; After hoisting:
  guard_type %x, Int -> deopt      ; executed once before loop
  loop:
    %r = int_add %x, 1
    ...
    goto loop
\end{verbatim}

Guard hoisting is safe as long as:
\begin{itemize}
  \item The guard dominates all uses of the guarded assumption.
  \item The guard is not moved past a side effect that could change the guarded property.
  \item The deoptimization at the hoisted point correctly reconstructs the program state.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Guard Combining}\label{sec:guard-combining}

Multiple guards can be combined into a single check using bitwise operations or branch-free code:

\begin{verbatim}
  ; Before: two separate guards
  guard_type %a, Int -> deopt_1
  guard_type %b, Int -> deopt_2

  ; After: combined guard (if type tags can be checked together)
  %combined = or %a.type_tag, %b.type_tag
  guard_eq %combined, INT_TAG_COMBINED -> deopt_combined
\end{verbatim}

% Annotated walkthrough
\subsection{Removing a Join Guard Without a Single Dominating Guard}

A guard can be redundant even if no individual guard dominates it. If each incoming edge carries the same fact on that edge's SSA value, the fact holds for the corresponding $\phi$ result. The check differs from merely asking whether some earlier instruction tested the same tag.

\textbf{Inputs and assumptions.} Consider an SSA $\phi$ with a precomputed must-fact set at the end of every incoming edge. The sets account for any operations that can invalidate a fact; for example, a call may invalidate a shape fact about a mutable object. The candidate guard is a pure check that deoptimizes only on failure. Code between the $\phi$ and the guard must also preserve the fact.

\begin{sudocode}[Prove a guard across incoming edges]
\Require Join value $v=\phi(\ldots)$, guard on fact $P(v)$
\Ensure Remove the guard only if failure is impossible
\If{$P$ might be invalidated between join and guard}
  \State \Return keep
\EndIf
\ForAll{incoming edges $e$ not proven unreachable}
  \Let{u}{\Call{PhiOperand}{v,e}}
  \If{$P(u)$ is not known to hold on $e$}
    \State \Return keep \Comment{One missing proof blocks removal}
  \EndIf
\EndFor
\State Replace the guard token by a proven fact
\State Delete the guard \Comment{Its failure state cannot be reached}
\end{sudocode}

\textbf{Example.} On the left arm, a successful integer guard proves that $a$ is tagged integer; on the right arm, a constant construction proves the same for $b=7$. At the join $v=\phi(a,b)$, a third integer guard is removable. If the right arm instead supplies an unclassified parameter, the third guard stays, even if profiling reports that the right arm was taken zero times.

\textbf{Invariant.} Any execution reaching the join took one particular predecessor and thus chose the operand whose must fact was checked. The guard cannot fail on that execution. The local test is linear in predecessor count with indexed facts, but it relies on the soundness of their upstream computation. Unknown reachability and unknown invalidations must not be treated as proof.

In \Cref{fig:visual-93}, the proof on each edge is attached to that
edge's operand, not to the name of the merged value.

