\section{Summary}\label{sec:spec-ir-summary}

Speculative and versioned IR extends the traditional compiler IR with the ability to express optimistic assumptions, guarded by runtime checks. This enables aggressive optimization of dynamically-typed and polymorphic code while maintaining correctness through deoptimization. The key design challenges are representing guards and assumptions in the IR, managing multiple code versions, handling guard failures correctly, and deciding which speculations are worthwhile based on profiling data.

\begin{historical}
Speculative optimization has its roots in the Self VM (1990s), which pioneered type feedback and inline caching. The concept of ``uncommon traps'' was formalized in the Java HotSpot compiler. The Graal compiler (2008--) and Truffle framework (2012--) pushed speculative optimization to its current state of the art, using a sea-of-nodes IR with rich guard and deoptimization support. The concept of versioned code for different assumptions has been explored in many systems, from the DylaDyn dynamic compilation framework to modern JavaScript JITs. The trend is toward more expressive speculative IRs that can capture a wider range of assumptions and more sophisticated profile-driven speculation strategies.
\end{historical}
